<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Bucharest (1812)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, signed on 28 May 1812, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1806–1812. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the eastern half of the Principality of Moldavia between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, which became the Russian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> gained limited autonomy. The peace freed Russian troops just before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of Russia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bucharest-1812.jpg" alt="Treaty of Bucharest (1812)" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Altenhof (KaterBegemot); derivative work by Durero · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Europe_1812_map_en.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1806 to 1812 began while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> were spreading across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A French move into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to send troops into Moldavia and Wallachia, and the sultan declared war. An Ottoman offensive against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> failed, and Russia destroyed the Ottoman fleet at the Battle of Athos. In 1811 Grand Vizier Ahmed Pasha gathered 60,000 soldiers in Ottoman Bulgaria, but the Russians led his army into an ambush and surrounded it. Soon afterwards Russia chose to make peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Negotiations opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> on 11 January 1812. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at first wanted more land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and the British plenipotentiary Stratford Canning advised it to moderate its demands, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> might otherwise turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The treaty was signed in Manuc's Inn on 28 May 1812 and ratified on 5 July 1812. The Ottoman Empire, to which Moldavia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, ceded to Russia the Budjak and the eastern half of the Principality of Moldavia between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> rivers, an area of 45,630 square kilometres that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. Russia also obtained trading rights on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> gave up their claims to most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> by accepting the Russian annexation of the Kingdom of Imereti in 1810, and kept Akhalkalaki, Poti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a>. Article 8 provided a truce with the rebelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and gave autonomy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The treaty was signed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> by Mikhail Kutuzov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had extricated themselves from a potentially disastrous war with a slight loss of territory, while the Russians were free for the coming war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the treaty became the basis for later Russo-Ottoman relations. In 1914 a statue of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> was inaugurated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Kishinev</a>, to mark a hundred years since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and it was demolished in 1918 after Bessarabia's union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Bucharest (1812)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1775 Austria annexed the north-west, which became Bukovina, and by the <b>Treaty of Bucharest</b> of 1812 Russia took everything east of the Pruth, nearly half the principality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When Russia made peace at <b>Bucharest</b> in 1812 to face Napoleon, the Ottomans reconquered Serbia in 1813 and Karađorđe fled to Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
